rail.prompt:
You are a code reviewer tasked with identifying "Blob" technical debt in the provided code snippets.
Please review the code snippet and identify any instances of Blob technical debt.

Below, you will find a definition of "Blob" technical debt along with example code snippets. 
Each example pair consists of a "Bad Example" that includes "Blob" technical debt and a "Good Example" where the technical debt has been resolved.

######################################################
Technical Debt: Blob - A Blob, also referred to as a God Class, Large Class, or Brain Class, is a class that centralizes too much functionality. 
        It often becomes overly complex, containing an excessive number of fields and methods, and performing too many responsibilities that should ideally be distributed across multiple smaller, more cohesive classes. 
        Such classes violate the Single Responsibility Principle and make the codebase difficult to understand, maintain, and extend. 
        Blob classes increase the risk of bugs and reduce the overall readability and modularity of the software. 
        To address this, Blob classes are refactored by extracting related responsibilities into smaller, well-defined classes and promote better object-oriented practices.

Bad Example 1.1: Blob

class SystemManager {
    private List<User> users;
    private List<Product> products;
    private List<Order> orders;

    // User-related methods
    public void addUser(String name) {
        // logic to add user
    }

    public void removeUser(String name) {
        // logic to remove user
    }

    // Product-related methods
    public void addProduct(String productName, double price) {
        // logic to add product
    }

    public void updateProductPrice(String productName, double newPrice) {
        // logic to update product price
    }

    // Order-related methods
    public void placeOrder(String userName, String productName) {
        // logic to place order
    }

    public void cancelOrder(String orderId) {
        // logic to cancel order
    }

    public void printSummary() {
        // logic to print a detailed summary of all users, products, and orders
    }
}

    - Issue: The SystemManager class is a Blob, as it manages too many responsibilities related to users, products, and orders. This leads to code that is hard to understand and maintain.

Good Example 1.2: No Blob

class UserManager {
    private List<User> users;

    public void addUser(String name) {
        // logic to add user
    }

    public void removeUser(String name) {
        // logic to remove user
    }
}

class ProductManager {
    private List<Product> products;

    public void addProduct(String productName, double price) {
        // logic to add product
    }

    public void updateProductPrice(String productName, double newPrice) {
        // logic to update product price
    }
}

class OrderManager {
    private List<Order> orders;

    public void placeOrder(String userName, String productName) {
        // logic to place order
    }

    public void cancelOrder(String orderId) {
        // logic to cancel order
    }
}

    - Benefit: Responsibilities of the SystemManager class have been split into three separate classes: UserManager, ProductManager, and OrderManager. 
        This refactoring improves cohesion and makes each class easier to understand and maintain.

Bad Example 1.3: Blob

class MegaUtility {
    // Fields related to multiple domains
    private List<Document> documents;
    private List<Report> reports;
    private List<LogEntry> logs;
    private DatabaseConnection dbConnection;
    private EmailService emailService;

    // Methods for managing documents
    public void createDocument(String title, String content) {
        // logic to create a document
    }

    public void deleteDocument(String title) {
        // logic to delete a document
    }

    // Methods for generating reports
    public void generateMonthlyReport() {
        // logic to generate report
    }

    public void printReportSummary() {
        // logic to print a report summary
    }

    // Methods for handling logs
    public void addLogEntry(String message) {
        // logic to add a log entry
    }

    public void clearLogs() {
        // logic to clear all log entries
    }

    // Methods for database operations
    public void connectToDatabase() {
        // logic to connect to a database
    }

    public void disconnectFromDatabase() {
        // logic to disconnect from the database
    }

    // Email-related methods
    public void sendEmail(String recipient, String message) {
        // logic to send an email
    }
}

    -Issue: The MegaUtility class is a prime example of a Blob class, as it handles operations related to documents, reports, logging, database connections, and email services. 
        The class is doing too much and should be refactored.

Good Example 1.4: No Blob

class DocumentManager {
    private List<Document> documents;

    public void createDocument(String title, String content) {
        // logic to create a document
    }

    public void deleteDocument(String title) {
        // logic to delete a document
    }
}

class ReportGenerator {
    private List<Report> reports;

    public void generateMonthlyReport() {
        // logic to generate report
    }

    public void printReportSummary() {
        // logic to print a report summary
    }
}

class LogManager {
    private List<LogEntry> logs;

    public void addLogEntry(String message) {
        // logic to add a log entry
    }

    public void clearLogs() {
        // logic to clear all log entries
    }
}

class DatabaseManager {
    private DatabaseConnection dbConnection;

    public void connectToDatabase() {
        // logic to connect to a database
    }

    public void disconnectFromDatabase() {
        // logic to disconnect from the database
    }
}

class EmailService {
    public void sendEmail(String recipient, String message) {
        // logic to send an email
    }
}

    - Benefit: The MegaUtility class has been broken down into specialized classes: DocumentManager, ReportGenerator, LogManager, DatabaseManager, and EmailService. 
        Each class now has a clear, focused responsibility, improving maintainability and readability.

